  "text": "**Solution 2 is more likely correct**, though I could not verify it against the literature, so my confidence is moderate.\n\n**Solution 1 ($\\alpha = -1$)** rests on a hazard model that doesn't hold up. It treats the survival of each point as a single one-dimensional integral over loop scales, with the hazard $c f(L')/L'$ and no separate account of the two arms that meet at a point. Its $f(L)=1/(1+c\\log(n/L))$ form is asserted rather than derived from the non-intersection structure in $d=4$, where that structure is what sets the exponent. Its $\\log^{-1}$ survival probability is also much thinner than the other known scalings in this setting.\n\n**Solution 2 ($\\alpha = -1/4$)** uses a framework that can be checked against a known result. The square-root non-intersection rule in $d=4$, with density exponent $b$ and $\\mu \\asymp (\\log n)^{1-b_1-b_2}$, reproduces Lawler's chronological LERW exponent $b = 1/3$ (length $n(\\log n)^{-1/3}$). Applying the same rule with both arms carrying the LLF density gives $4b = 1$, so $b = 1/4$. The argument that both arms are already thinned at the relevant scales is plausible because erasure runs largest-first. This also matches my recollection of Lawler's work on largest-loop-first erasure in $d=4$, which I believe gives $n(\\log n)^{-1/4+o(1)}$, but I cannot confirm that recollection here.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
